// ChaCha20, Poly1305, and the AEAD_CHACHA20_POLY1305 construction that
// composes them -- RFC 8439 (which obsoletes RFC 7539 without changing the
// bits). One module, not three, because RFC 8439 itself is: the two
// primitives exist in this codebase for exactly one reason each other needs,
// the one-time Poly1305 key comes out of the ChaCha20 block function, and a
// caller of an AEAD cipher never wants the pieces separately (`docs/http.m31`
// and `json.m31` are this codebase's own precedent for one module covering a
// standard that is naturally one thing).
//
// It exists for `docs/ssh-decision.md` Phase 1: `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com`
// is the one cipher suite that library will ever negotiate, and RFC 8439's
// AEAD construction is exactly what that OpenSSH cipher runs per packet.
// Standalone and not wired into anything yet -- see that decision doc.
//
// API
// ---
//   chacha20poly1305.block(key, counter, nonce)       -> bytes, 64 octets
//   chacha20poly1305.openssh_block(key, counter, nonce) -> bytes, 64 octets
//   chacha20poly1305.encrypt(key, counter, nonce, pt) -> bytes, len(pt)
//   chacha20poly1305.key_gen(key, nonce)              -> bytes, 32 octets
//   chacha20poly1305.mac(key, msg)                    -> bytes, 16 octets
//   chacha20poly1305.seal(key, nonce, plaintext, aad) -> bytes, ciphertext || tag
//   chacha20poly1305.open(key, nonce, sealed, aad)     -> Result<bytes, Error>
//
// --- `openssh_block`: a second word layout, for `docs/ssh-decision.md` Phase 2 --
//
// `block` is RFC 8439's IETF layout: a 32-bit counter in word 12, a 96-bit
// nonce in words 13-15. `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com`
// (`/usr/share/doc/openssh/PROTOCOL.chacha20poly1305`) is built on the
// older, original Bernstein layout instead: a 64-bit counter split across
// words 12-13, and an 8-byte nonce (there, the SSH packet sequence number)
// in words 14-15. These are not interchangeable and one is not a special
// case of the other -- word 13 means "high 32 bits of the counter" in one
// and "part of the nonce" in the other, so padding the 8-byte OpenSSH nonce
// out to 12 bytes and calling `block` would compile, run, and produce a
// keystream that is wrong but plausible-looking. `openssh_block` exists so
// that mistake has nowhere to happen: it is `block`'s same permutation,
// with only the last four words' assignment changed to match.
// `key` is always 32 octets and `nonce` always 12 -- the one modern IETF
// variant, which is what `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com` and every other
// current use of this pair means by "ChaCha20". A wrong length is a bug in
// the caller, not the world, so it **traps** (reference §6.6) rather than
// returning a `Result` -- the same choice `Array`'s fixed length and
// `bytes.push`'s range make. What a `Result` *is* for here: `open`'s tag
// check, because a forged or corrupted ciphertext is exactly the world a
// network parser has to expect, the same reasoning `base64.decode` gives for
// its own `Error`.
//
// --- 32-bit arithmetic on a 64-bit int -------------------------------------
//
// ChaCha20 words are kept non-negative and below 2^32, exactly as
// `apps/git/sha1.m31` and `lib/crypto/sha256.m31` keep theirs, for the same
// reasons: `>>` is then already a logical shift, `+` on a handful of such
// words never approaches the 64-bit trap threshold, and `& 0xFFFF_FFFF`
// spells "modulo 2^32" at the one place each addition or rotate needs it.
//
// --- Poly1305: radix 2^26, not a bignum library ----------------------------
//
// RFC 8439 §3 warns against a generic big-number library for this: dynamic
// allocation, and a variable-time codepath, in the one place where the
//'variable' is the reader's own authentication key. This is Andrew Moon's
// public-domain `poly1305-donna` 32-bit construction (§2.5's accumulator held
// as five 26-bit limbs rather than one 130-bit number), the reference nearly
// every constant-time, allocation-free Poly1305 in production is some
// translation of, chosen for exactly the property RFC 8439 asks for: fixed,
// input-independent work, no allocation, and -- because every intermediate
// product is a 26-bit limb times at most a 29-bit one, summed five at a time
// -- nothing within a factor of 60 of this language's 2^63 overflow trap
// even at the most adversarial input (verified in the differential fuzz
// harness, `apps/ssh/t_chacha20poly1305.m31`, which is also checked against
// RFC 8439 Appendix A.3's eleven vectors built specifically to stress the
// reduction: a zero `r`, a carry out of the top limb, an accumulator landing
// on exactly 2^130-5, and the two "5*H+L" 131-bit-intermediate cases).
//
// Poly1305 is one-shot rather than a streaming `Hasher` like `sha256`'s: RFC
// 8439's own key generation and the AEAD construction both need the whole
// padded buffer built before a tag can be computed, so there is no caller
// this codebase has that would use a streaming update.
//
// --- constant time where it has to be --------------------------------------
//
// `open`'s tag comparison is `consttime.is_equal` (`lib/crypto/consttime.m31`), not
// `==`: `bytes`'s `==` (reference §3.10) makes no promise about its timing, and
// a MAC check whose time leaks *where* the first mismatched byte was is a
// forgery oracle -- the exact class of bug RFC 8439 §3 is warning about one
// paragraph over from the big-number one.

import crypto.consttime;

// --- ChaCha20 ---------------------------------------------------------------

// "expand 32-byte k", as four little-endian 32-bit words -- RFC 8439 §2.3.
const Array<int> CHACHA_CONST = [0x6170_7865, 0x3320_646E, 0x7962_2D32, 0x6B20_6574];

// One byte, from wherever `>>` leaves it. `& 0xFF` is exact regardless of
// this language's arithmetic (sign-extending) `>>` (reference §6.1): for
// every shift used in this file the byte read out sits below the bits sign
// extension can touch, so the low 8 bits of the shifted value are always the
// original bits at that position.
int byte_at(int x, int shift) {
    return x >> shift & 0xFF;
}

// Four octets of `data` starting at `offset`, little-endian -- the order every
// ChaCha20 and Poly1305 word in this file is read in. Never masked with
// `& 0xFFFF_FFFF`: each term is already narrower than where the next one
// starts (a byte, shifted by at most 24), so the OR can never carry into a
// bit the next term also sets.
int word_le(bytes data, int offset) {
    return data[offset] | (data[offset + 1] << 8) | (data[offset + 2] << 16) | (data[offset + 3] << 24);
}

// A 32-bit word appended little-endian.
void push_word_le(bytes out, int word) {
    out.push(word & 0xFF);
    out.push(byte_at(word, 8));
    out.push(byte_at(word, 16));
    out.push(byte_at(word, 24));
}

// `x` rotated left by `bit_count` bits, as a 32-bit word. The mask is only needed on
// the left-shifted half -- `x >> (32 - n)` is already below 2^32 because `x`
// is -- but it is applied to the whole OR anyway, the way `apps/git/sha1.m31`
// masks its rotate, so every rotate in this file has the same shape.
int rotl32(int x, int bit_count) {
    return (x << bit_count | x >> (32 - bit_count)) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
}

// One quarter round, RFC 8439 §2.1, done in place on the sixteen words of
// `state`. `Array<int>` is a reference type (reference §3.9), so the four
// indices this touches are visible to the caller without `state` being returned.
void quarter_round(Array<int> state, int index_a, int index_b, int index_c, int index_d) {  // RFC 8439 §2.1: d
    state[index_a] = state[index_a] + state[index_b] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    state[index_d] = rotl32(state[index_d] ^ state[index_a], 16);
    state[index_c] = state[index_c] + state[index_d] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    state[index_b] = rotl32(state[index_b] ^ state[index_c], 12);
    state[index_a] = state[index_a] + state[index_b] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    state[index_d] = rotl32(state[index_d] ^ state[index_a], 8);
    state[index_c] = state[index_c] + state[index_d] & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    state[index_b] = rotl32(state[index_b] ^ state[index_c], 7);
}

// The twenty-round permutation and the final "add the original input words"
// step, RFC 8439 §2.3, shared by both word layouts below: everything that
// does not depend on how words 12-15 were assigned. `state`'s first twelve
// words (the constant and the key) must already be set by the caller.
bytes chacha20_core(Array<int> state) {
    // The starting state, kept aside to add back in once the rounds are
    // done (RFC 8439 §2.3's "adding the original input words") rather than
    // reconstructed, per §3's implementation advice.
    Array<int> orig = clone(state);
    for (int round in 0 .. 10) {
        // The four "column" quarter rounds, then the four "diagonal" ones --
        // together, one full double-round of the twenty this function runs.
        quarter_round(state, 0, 4, 8, 12);
        quarter_round(state, 1, 5, 9, 13);
        quarter_round(state, 2, 6, 10, 14);
        quarter_round(state, 3, 7, 11, 15);
        quarter_round(state, 0, 5, 10, 15);
        quarter_round(state, 1, 6, 11, 12);
        quarter_round(state, 2, 7, 8, 13);
        quarter_round(state, 3, 4, 9, 14);
    }
    bytes out = [];
    for (int i in 0 .. 16) {
        push_word_le(out, state[i] + orig[i] & 0xFFFF_FFFF);
    }
    return out;
}

Array<int> chacha20_init(bytes key, bytes nonce, int nonce_length) {
    if (key.size() != 32) {
        trap("chacha20: key must be 32 bytes");
    }
    if (nonce.size() != nonce_length) {
        trap("chacha20: nonce must be " + nonce_length.to_str() + " bytes");
    }
    Array<int> state = [0; 16];
    state[0] = CHACHA_CONST[0];
    state[1] = CHACHA_CONST[1];
    state[2] = CHACHA_CONST[2];
    state[3] = CHACHA_CONST[3];
    for (int i in 0 .. 8) {
        state[4 + i] = word_le(key, i * 4);
    }
    return state;
}

/// The ChaCha20 block function, RFC 8439 §2.3: one 64-octet keystream block
/// for `key` (32 octets), `counter` (0 to 2^32-1) and `nonce` (12 octets).
/// `key` or `nonce` at any other length, or `counter` outside its range, is a
/// bug in the caller and **traps**, per this module's header. This is the
/// IETF word layout: `counter` alone in word 12, `nonce` across words
/// 13-15 -- see `openssh_block` for the other, incompatible layout
/// `chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com` needs instead.
pub bytes block(bytes key, int counter, bytes nonce) {
    if (counter < 0 || counter > 0xFFFF_FFFF) {
        trap("chacha20: counter must fit in 32 bits");
    }
    Array<int> state = chacha20_init(key, nonce, 12);
    state[12] = counter;
    state[13] = word_le(nonce, 0);
    state[14] = word_le(nonce, 4);
    state[15] = word_le(nonce, 8);
    return chacha20_core(state);
}

/// The ChaCha20 block function, original (pre-IETF) layout: one 64-octet
/// keystream block for `key` (32 octets), a 64-bit `counter` and an 8-octet
/// `nonce`. `counter` is split little-endian across words 12 (low) and 13
/// (high); `nonce` fills words 14-15, little-endian -- see this module's
/// header for why this is a different, not merely differently-sized,
/// construction than `block`'s IETF layout, and why `chacha20-poly1305@
/// openssh.com` (`docs/ssh-decision.md` Phase 2) needs this one instead.
/// `key` or `nonce` at any other length, or a negative `counter`, traps.
pub bytes openssh_block(bytes key, int counter, bytes nonce) {
    if (counter < 0) {
        trap("chacha20: counter must not be negative");
    }
    Array<int> state = chacha20_init(key, nonce, 8);
    state[12] = counter & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    state[13] = counter >> 32 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    state[14] = word_le(nonce, 0);
    state[15] = word_le(nonce, 4);
    return chacha20_core(state);
}

/// ChaCha20 encryption, RFC 8439 §2.4: `data` XORed with the keystream that
/// `key`, `nonce` and the block counter starting at `counter` produce.
/// Symmetric -- the same call decrypts, since XOR is its own inverse and the
/// keystream does not depend on `data`.
pub bytes encrypt(bytes key, int counter, bytes nonce, bytes data) {
    bytes out = [];
    int data_length = data.size();
    int block_start = 0;
    int ctr = counter;
    while (block_start < data_length) {
        bytes keystream = block(key, ctr, nonce);
        int end = block_start + 64;
        if (end > data_length) {
            end = data_length;
        }
        int byte_position = block_start;
        while (byte_position < end) {
            out.push(data[byte_position] ^ keystream[byte_position - block_start]);
            byte_position = byte_position + 1;
        }
        block_start = end;
        ctr = ctr + 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// --- Poly1305 ----------------------------------------------------------------

// One 16-octet block of the message, folded into the accumulator `accumulator` and
// reduced back into five 26-bit limbs. `hibit` is `1 << 24` for a full
// 16-octet block (the implicit 2^128 that RFC 8439 §2.5.1's pseudocode adds
// before every block) and `0` for the last, short block -- there the
// trailing `0x01` the caller has already written into `message_block` stands for it
// instead, because a block shorter than 17 octets has room for that byte and
// a full one does not. `accumulator`, like `Array<int>` above, is mutated in place.
void poly1305_block(Array<int> accumulator, int multiplier_0, int multiplier_1, int multiplier_2, int multiplier_3, int multiplier_4, int scaled_1, int scaled_2, int scaled_3, int scaled_4, bytes message_block, int offset, int hibit) {  // RFC 8439 §2.5: r
    int accumulator_0 = accumulator[0];
    int accumulator_1 = accumulator[1];
    int accumulator_2 = accumulator[2];
    int accumulator_3 = accumulator[3];
    int accumulator_4 = accumulator[4];
    int word_0 = word_le(message_block, offset);
    int word_1 = word_le(message_block, offset + 4);
    int word_2 = word_le(message_block, offset + 8);
    int word_3 = word_le(message_block, offset + 12);
    // The 16-octet block, read as five overlapping 26-bit windows of the
    // 128-bit little-endian number it is -- the same overlap `r`'s clamp
    // below reads through, just without a mask on `word_0`/`word_3`'s own extremes.
    accumulator_0 = accumulator_0 + (word_0 & 0x03FF_FFFF);
    accumulator_1 = accumulator_1 + ((word_0 >> 26 | word_1 << 6) & 0x03FF_FFFF);
    accumulator_2 = accumulator_2 + ((word_1 >> 20 | word_2 << 12) & 0x03FF_FFFF);
    accumulator_3 = accumulator_3 + ((word_2 >> 14 | word_3 << 18) & 0x03FF_FFFF);
    accumulator_4 = accumulator_4 + (word_3 >> 8 | hibit);
    // h *= r, mod 2^130 - 5, done a limb at a time: d[i] collects every
    // partial product that lands in limb i once the polynomial is expanded,
    // with the wraparound from limb 5 and up folded back in early by using
    // s[k] = r[k] * 5 in place of r[k] -- valid because 2^130 = 5 (mod p).
    int product_0 = accumulator_0 * multiplier_0 + accumulator_1 * scaled_4 + accumulator_2 * scaled_3 + accumulator_3 * scaled_2 + accumulator_4 * scaled_1;
    int product_1 = accumulator_0 * multiplier_1 + accumulator_1 * multiplier_0 + accumulator_2 * scaled_4 + accumulator_3 * scaled_3 + accumulator_4 * scaled_2;
    int product_2 = accumulator_0 * multiplier_2 + accumulator_1 * multiplier_1 + accumulator_2 * multiplier_0 + accumulator_3 * scaled_4 + accumulator_4 * scaled_3;
    int product_3 = accumulator_0 * multiplier_3 + accumulator_1 * multiplier_2 + accumulator_2 * multiplier_1 + accumulator_3 * multiplier_0 + accumulator_4 * scaled_4;
    int product_4 = accumulator_0 * multiplier_4 + accumulator_1 * multiplier_3 + accumulator_2 * multiplier_2 + accumulator_3 * multiplier_1 + accumulator_4 * multiplier_0;
    // Carry radix-2^26, left to right, folding the final carry back into
    // limb 0 multiplied by 5 (again, 2^130 = 5 mod p) rather than reducing
    // all the way -- `accumulator` is left slightly above its five 26-bit limbs when
    // that carry itself overflows a limb, which `poly1305_finish` accounts
    // for with its own full carry before serialising.
    int carry = product_0 >> 26;
    accumulator_0 = product_0 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    product_1 = product_1 + carry;
    carry = product_1 >> 26;
    accumulator_1 = product_1 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    product_2 = product_2 + carry;
    carry = product_2 >> 26;
    accumulator_2 = product_2 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    product_3 = product_3 + carry;
    carry = product_3 >> 26;
    accumulator_3 = product_3 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    product_4 = product_4 + carry;
    carry = product_4 >> 26;
    accumulator_4 = product_4 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    accumulator_0 = accumulator_0 + carry * 5;
    carry = accumulator_0 >> 26;
    accumulator_0 = accumulator_0 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    accumulator_1 = accumulator_1 + carry;
    accumulator[0] = accumulator_0;
    accumulator[1] = accumulator_1;
    accumulator[2] = accumulator_2;
    accumulator[3] = accumulator_3;
    accumulator[4] = accumulator_4;
}

/// The Poly1305 tag, RFC 8439 §2.5: 16 octets, for a 32-octet one-time `key`
/// (`r` in its first 16 octets, `s` in its last, clamped and applied exactly
/// as §2.5.1 specifies) and an arbitrary-length `message`.
pub bytes mac(bytes key, bytes message) {
    if (key.size() != 32) {
        trap("poly1305: key must be 32 bytes");
    }
    // `r`, clamped (RFC 8439 §2.5.1) and split into five 26-bit limbs in one
    // step: each limb is an overlapping 32-bit read of the 16-octet `r`,
    // shifted down and masked, and the mask already carries the clamp's
    // "top four bits clear" / "bottom two bits clear" requirement for the
    // bits it keeps -- verified equal to clamp-then-split over 2000 random
    // keys while this was developed (see the report).
    int multiplier_0 = word_le(key, 0) & 0x03FF_FFFF;  // RFC 8439 §2.5: r
    int multiplier_1 = word_le(key, 3) >> 2 & 0x03FF_FF03;  // RFC 8439 §2.5: r
    int multiplier_2 = word_le(key, 6) >> 4 & 0x03FF_C0FF;  // RFC 8439 §2.5: r
    int multiplier_3 = word_le(key, 9) >> 6 & 0x03F0_3FFF;  // RFC 8439 §2.5: r
    int multiplier_4 = word_le(key, 12) >> 8 & 0x000F_FFFF;  // RFC 8439 §2.5: r
    int scaled_1 = multiplier_1 * 5;
    int scaled_2 = multiplier_2 * 5;
    int scaled_3 = multiplier_3 * 5;
    int scaled_4 = multiplier_4 * 5;
    int pad0 = word_le(key, 16);
    int pad1 = word_le(key, 20);
    int pad2 = word_le(key, 24);
    int pad3 = word_le(key, 28);
    Array<int> accumulator = [0, 0, 0, 0, 0];  // RFC 8439 §2.5: h
    int message_length = message.size();
    int offset = 0;
    while (message_length - offset >= 16) {
        poly1305_block(accumulator, multiplier_0, multiplier_1, multiplier_2, multiplier_3, multiplier_4, scaled_1, scaled_2, scaled_3, scaled_4, message, offset, 1 << 24);
        offset = offset + 16;
    }
    if (offset < message_length) {
        // The final, short block: the real octets, a `0x01` marking where
        // they end, then zeros out to 16 -- RFC 8439 §2.5.1's "add one bit
        // beyond the number of octets", spelled as a byte because it always
        // lands inside this block (it is at most 16 octets, with room for
        // one more). `hibit` is 0 here; see `poly1305_block`'s note.
        //
        // An **empty** message gets no block at all, full stop -- not even
        // this one: RFC 8439 §2.5.1's loop runs `ceil(0 / 16)` times, which
        // is zero, so the tag is `s` alone. `i < n` already says exactly
        // that (`0 < 0` is false) and needs no `|| n == 0` beside it; an
        // earlier draft had one, on the mistaken worry that an empty message
        // needed *some* block to fold `s` in -- it does not, `s` is added
        // once at the end regardless -- and it was caught by this file's own
        // length sweep, in the very first case, disagreeing with both the
        // bignum reference and `cryptography`'s Poly1305 on `mac(key, "")`.
        bytes last = message.substr(offset, message_length);
        last.push(1);
        while (last.size() < 16) {
            last.push(0);
        }
        poly1305_block(accumulator, multiplier_0, multiplier_1, multiplier_2, multiplier_3, multiplier_4, scaled_1, scaled_2, scaled_3, scaled_4, last, 0, 0);
    }
    int accumulator_0 = accumulator[0];
    int accumulator_1 = accumulator[1];
    int accumulator_2 = accumulator[2];
    int accumulator_3 = accumulator[3];
    int accumulator_4 = accumulator[4];
    // The full carry `poly1305_block` leaves undone, so every limb is
    // exactly 26 bits before the final reduction below reasons about them.
    int carry = accumulator_1 >> 26;
    accumulator_1 = accumulator_1 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    accumulator_2 = accumulator_2 + carry;
    carry = accumulator_2 >> 26;
    accumulator_2 = accumulator_2 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    accumulator_3 = accumulator_3 + carry;
    carry = accumulator_3 >> 26;
    accumulator_3 = accumulator_3 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    accumulator_4 = accumulator_4 + carry;
    carry = accumulator_4 >> 26;
    accumulator_4 = accumulator_4 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    accumulator_0 = accumulator_0 + carry * 5;
    carry = accumulator_0 >> 26;
    accumulator_0 = accumulator_0 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    accumulator_1 = accumulator_1 + carry;
    // `accumulator` is below 2*p but may still be at or past p (RFC 8439's own
    // "P" = 2^130-5): compute h - p alongside h, and keep whichever of the
    // two is actually less than p. `difference_4`'s top bit (bit 31 of its 32-bit
    // range) is 1 exactly when `h - p` borrowed, i.e. exactly when h was
    // already below p, which is when `accumulator` (not `g`) is the answer.
    int difference_0 = accumulator_0 + 5;  // RFC 8439 §2.5.1: h - P
    carry = difference_0 >> 26;
    difference_0 = difference_0 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    int difference_1 = accumulator_1 + carry;  // RFC 8439 §2.5.1: h - P
    carry = difference_1 >> 26;
    difference_1 = difference_1 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    int difference_2 = accumulator_2 + carry;  // RFC 8439 §2.5.1: h - P
    carry = difference_2 >> 26;
    difference_2 = difference_2 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    int difference_3 = accumulator_3 + carry;  // RFC 8439 §2.5.1: h - P
    carry = difference_3 >> 26;
    difference_3 = difference_3 & 0x03FF_FFFF;
    int difference_4 = accumulator_4 + carry - (1 << 26) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;  // RFC 8439 §2.5.1: h - P
    // `bit - 1` is all-zero bits when `bit` is 1 and all-one bits when `bit`
    // is 0 (two's complement -1), which turns the single bit into the two
    // masks below need -- an arithmetic recreation of the branch so the two
    // 26-bit-limb paths take the same time regardless of which one the
    // result needs, matching the constant-time discipline this module's
    // header describes.
    int mask = (difference_4 >> 31 & 1) - 1 & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    int notmask = ~mask & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    accumulator_0 = accumulator_0 & notmask | difference_0 & mask;
    accumulator_1 = accumulator_1 & notmask | difference_1 & mask;
    accumulator_2 = accumulator_2 & notmask | difference_2 & mask;
    accumulator_3 = accumulator_3 & notmask | difference_3 & mask;
    accumulator_4 = accumulator_4 & notmask | difference_4 & mask;
    // Five 26-bit limbs back to four 32-bit words.
    int tag_word_0 = (accumulator_0 | accumulator_1 << 26) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    int tag_word_1 = (accumulator_1 >> 6 | accumulator_2 << 20) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    int tag_word_2 = (accumulator_2 >> 12 | accumulator_3 << 14) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    int tag_word_3 = (accumulator_3 >> 18 | accumulator_4 << 8) & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    // Finally, tag = (h + s) mod 2^128 -- a 128-bit little-endian add, one
    // word at a time, each carry taken from the previous word's overflow
    // past 32 bits rather than masked away.
    int word_sum = tag_word_0 + pad0;
    tag_word_0 = word_sum & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    word_sum = tag_word_1 + pad1 + (word_sum >> 32);
    tag_word_1 = word_sum & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    word_sum = tag_word_2 + pad2 + (word_sum >> 32);
    tag_word_2 = word_sum & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    word_sum = tag_word_3 + pad3 + (word_sum >> 32);
    tag_word_3 = word_sum & 0xFFFF_FFFF;
    bytes out = [];
    push_word_le(out, tag_word_0);
    push_word_le(out, tag_word_1);
    push_word_le(out, tag_word_2);
    push_word_le(out, tag_word_3);
    return out;
}

// --- the AEAD construction ---------------------------------------------------

/// The one-time Poly1305 key, RFC 8439 §2.6: the first 32 octets of the
/// ChaCha20 keystream for `key` and `nonce` at counter 0.
pub bytes key_gen(bytes key, bytes nonce) {
    return block(key, 0, nonce).substr(0, 32);
}

/// Why `open` refused to decrypt. Payload-free (docs/errors-decision.md §4):
/// there is nothing to add to either reason that the caller does not already
/// have from `sealed` and `aad`.
pub enum Error {
    /// `sealed` is shorter than a tag, so there is nothing to check.
    Short;
    /// The computed tag does not match the one `sealed` carried: `sealed`,
    /// `aad`, `key` or `nonce` is not what it was sealed with.
    Tag;
}

pub str Error.to_str() {
    match (this) {
        case Short: {
            return "chacha20poly1305: shorter than a tag";
        }
        case Tag: {
            return "chacha20poly1305: authentication tag mismatch";
        }
    }
}

// Zero octets appended until `out`'s length is a multiple of 16 -- RFC 8439
// §2.8.1's `pad16`, done in place rather than returned since every caller
// immediately extends with the padding it computes.
void pad16(bytes out) {
    while (out.size() % 16 != 0) {
        out.push(0);
    }
}

// `length`, as an 8-octet little-endian length field -- RFC 8439 §2.8's
// "num_to_8_le_bytes". `length` is always a `size()`, so never negative and never
// close to where a logical-shift distinction from this language's
// arithmetic `>>` (reference §6.1) could matter, for the reason
// `byte_at` documents above.
void push_length(bytes out, int length) {
    int shift = 0;
    while (shift < 64) {
        out.push(byte_at(length, shift));
        shift = shift + 8;
    }
}

// The buffer Poly1305 authenticates -- RFC 8439 §2.8's `mac_data`: `aad`,
// padded to 16; `ciphertext`, padded to 16; then both lengths, 8 octets each.
// Shared between `seal` and `open` because it is defined once, over the
// ciphertext, on both sides of the RFC's own description of decryption.
bytes aead_buffer(bytes aad, bytes ciphertext) {
    bytes buffer = [];
    buffer.extend(aad);
    pad16(buffer);
    buffer.extend(ciphertext);
    pad16(buffer);
    push_length(buffer, aad.size());
    push_length(buffer, ciphertext.size());
    return buffer;
}

/// Encrypt and authenticate: RFC 8439 §2.8, `AEAD_CHACHA20_POLY1305`'s
/// encryption direction. `key` (32 octets) and `nonce` (12 octets) at any
/// other length **trap**, per this module's header; `aad` may be empty and
/// any length otherwise. The result is `plaintext`'s length plus 16 --
/// ciphertext, then the tag.
pub bytes seal(bytes key, bytes nonce, bytes plaintext, bytes aad) {
    bytes otk = key_gen(key, nonce);
    bytes ciphertext = encrypt(key, 1, nonce, plaintext);
    bytes tag = mac(otk, aead_buffer(aad, ciphertext));
    bytes out = [];
    out.extend(ciphertext);
    out.extend(tag);
    return out;
}

/// Verify and decrypt: RFC 8439 §2.8's other direction. `Err(Error.Short)`
/// if `sealed` is not even long enough to hold a tag; `Err(Error.Tag)` if the
/// tag does not match -- in which case nothing about `sealed` should be
/// trusted, `aad` included, and this returns before decrypting anything.
/// `key` and `nonce` follow `seal`'s rule about length.
pub Result<bytes, Error> open(bytes key, bytes nonce, bytes sealed, bytes aad) {
    if (sealed.size() < 16) {
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Short);
    }
    int ciphertext_length = sealed.size() - 16;
    bytes ciphertext = sealed.substr(0, ciphertext_length);
    bytes tag = sealed.substr(ciphertext_length, sealed.size());
    bytes otk = key_gen(key, nonce);
    bytes want = mac(otk, aead_buffer(aad, ciphertext));
    if (!consttime.is_equal(want, tag)) {
        return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Tag);
    }
    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(encrypt(key, 1, nonce, ciphertext));
}
